Lexifina's own work in this repository (the arrangement of the history, the file layout, the commit messages, the
tables and the tools) is dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode

The legal texts are Nigerian federal legislation. Official texts of legislation are not protected by copyright in
Nigeria. Copyright Act, 2022 (Act No. 8 of 2022), section 3: "The following shall not be eligible for copyright -
... (b) official texts of a legislative or administrative nature as well as any official translations, except their
compilations". Each Act is kept here as its own text, one folder per Act; no compilation's selection, notes or
apparatus is claimed or reproduced as such.

Sources and their terms:

- Policy and Legal Advocacy Centre (PLAC), "The Complete 2004 Laws of Nigeria" (https://placng.org/lawsofnigeria/):
  the Acts of the Laws of the Federation of Nigeria 2004, as PLAC's HTML pages and PDFs serve them (read 2026-11-26).
  The site states "(c) Copyright Policy and Legal Advocacy Centre (2021)" for the site and states no licence for the
  law texts; only the official texts of the Acts are taken, not PLAC's own writing.
- PLAC, Acts collection (https://placng.org/i/acts/): Acts of the National Assembly since 2003 and the Fifth
  Alteration Acts to the Constitution (2023), as PDFs (read 2026-11-26); same terms as above.
- Federal Ministry of Justice, Abuja: the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria, 1999, from its print
  "[With the First, Second and Third Alterations]" (justice.gov.ng, read through the Internet Archive's capture of
  15 October 2021 because the ministry no longer serves the file); no licence stated.
- Federal Government Printer, Lagos: Official Gazette copies of Acts (as listed in README.md and REPRODUCE.md),
  served by the National Assembly, the Securities and Exchange Commission, the Nigerian Communications Commission
  and the Corporate Affairs Commission; no licence stated.

Where a source PDF is a page scan with a text layer added by its publisher, the text is that publisher's OCR, as
served, and the commit says so (Text-source: publisher OCR). Lexifina ran no OCR.

Changes made by Lexifina: each Act was converted to one Markdown file per section or Schedule where the section
numbering could be read, otherwise one file; spacing was normalised; the versions were arranged as git commits dated
on the day each took effect. The wording of the law was not changed. Neither PLAC, the Federal Government Printer,
the Federal Ministry of Justice nor any website named here endorses this repository.

This repository is not legal advice and not an official publication.
